 \maketitle
 
 \begin{abstract}
-The goal is to reconstruct a continuous target-property profile along a well
-using dense auxiliary wireline logs and sparse observations of the target
-property at selected depths. Direct models can use those sparse observations
-as input features, yet the reconstruction need not honor them as measurements.
-We introduce Conditional Latent-Prior Compressed Sensing with Generative
-Models (CLP-CSGM). A learned decoder maps latent codes to candidate target-property
-windows, limiting which curves it can reconstruct. Dense auxiliary logs predict a
-conditional latent code, $z_0$. Latent refinement balances disagreement with
-the sparse measurements against proximity to $z_0$. That code is the
-reference for this balance, not only a starting value. A residual at the
-observed depths can remain. The local recovery bound remains conditional
-and does not show that the trained decoder separates nearby windows.
-The cross-well test uses public Dutch North Sea logs, with three development
-wells and one held-out well. Targets are Larionov clay volume from gamma ray
-and processed density-derived porosity. Sparse observations are subsamples of these curves,
-not independent core or laboratory measurements.
-CLP-CSGM has lower mean root mean square error (RMSE) than the direct
-autoencoder in seven of nine cross-well configurations, chiefly when training
-support is limited, but loses in two. For the gamma-ray-only case, $\rho$ denotes the nominal observed
-fraction of window depths. CLP-CSGM has lower mean
-RMSE at $\rho\in\{0.20,0.30\}$, whereas the autoencoder is lower at
-$\rho\in\{0.40,0.60\}$; their errors are nearly equal at $\rho=0.50$.
-
-At the reference noise standard deviation $0.01$, depth
-interpolation outperforms CLP-CSGM in all nine cross-well
-configurations. This ordering changes under larger perturbations that were
-not calibrated to field uncertainties. A laboratory-porosity supplement
-also favors the direct autoencoder in mean RMSE. These findings
-indicate regime-dependent gains relative to the direct autoencoder, rather
-than overall superiority, and broader validation remains necessary.
+We reconstruct a continuous target-property profile when dense auxiliary
+wireline logs are available but the target is observed at only a few depths.
+Direct predictors can accept those observations as features without retaining
+them as explicit measurements during inference. Conditional Latent-Prior
+Compressed Sensing with Generative Models (CLP-CSGM) instead combines a
+learned target-window representation, a latent anchor
+$\boldsymbol{z}_0$ predicted from
+the dense logs, and test-time refinement against the sparse measurements.
+Its local recovery bound is conditional and does not establish separation of
+nearby windows by the trained decoder.
+
+The cross-well evaluation uses three development wells and F03-4 as the
+held-out well. CLP-CSGM Ridge has lower mean root
+mean square error (RMSE) than a direct autoencoder in seven of nine
+configurations, especially at lower training support; it also improves on its
+conditional prior-only prediction in all
+nine. For the GR-only porosity case, $\rho$ denotes the nominal fraction of
+window depths selected for observation. CLP-CSGM is lower at
+$\rho\in\{0.20,0.30\}$, the autoencoder is lower at
+$\rho\in\{0.40,0.60\}$, and their errors are nearly equal at $\rho=0.50$.
+At the reference noise standard deviation $0.01$, depth interpolation has
+lower RMSE than CLP-CSGM in all nine cross-well configurations. The ordering
+becomes more favorable to CLP-CSGM under larger synthetic perturbations that
+were not calibrated to field uncertainties.
+
+The F3 targets are processed curves: clay volume is Larionov-derived from
+gamma ray, porosity is density-derived, and sparse observations are
+subsamples rather than independent core or laboratory measurements. The
+results support conditional benefits of explicit latent
+refinement in the evaluated regimes, not general superiority, and motivate
+broader validation with independently calibrated targets.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \keywords{compressed sensing; generative model; inverse problem;
